# Slack channels the lab reads or posts in: /srv/lab/slack.conf. The PI owns this file; agents don't edit it.
# Read by lab-slack (sync runs from hpc's crontab every 10 minutes) and lab-slack-listen.
#
# workspace <name> [token=<file>]      default token file: ~/.config/lab/slack-<name>.token (hpc only, chmod 600)
#   The listener also needs the app-level token in ~/.config/lab/slack-<name>.apptoken.
#
# pi <workspace> <slack user id>       the PI's own Slack user (profile -> ... -> Copy member ID). Only this
#   user may approve held cluster jobs by DM, and the PI's DMs reach the director. It also makes the
#   pseudo-channel <workspace>/@pi: a DM to the PI, never mirrored, that only the director posts to, and that
#   leads use only to send the PI their project summaries (lab-slack post --attach <pdf> --issue <summary issue>
#   [--then <channel>]). The PI answers a summary in its DM thread: `post` (or "yes, post it") shares it in
#   the --then channel, `no` drops it, anything else goes to the summary issue as the PI's comments. Which
#   channel each project's summaries go to is written in LAB-RULES.md ("Project summaries"); --then must be a
#   channel below that lists the project, in draft or post mode.
#
# channel <workspace> <channel> projects=<p1,p2> mode=read|draft|post [posters=lead,worker] [every=6h] [per_hour=4]
#   The app must also be invited to the channel (/invite @<bot>). Its history is mirrored only into the
#   listed projects' data/slack/ (outside git). read: no posting. draft: posts go to the PI's inbox for
#   approval first. post: posts go out directly, signed, logged, rate-limited. The mirror of a project on the
#   agent account is made readable by group agents (that project's folder only), so its agents can read it.
#   Posting a file (--attach) needs the bot scope files:write (slack/manifest.yaml has it).
#
# Mentions and direct messages (lab-slack-listen) are answered in any channel listed here, whatever its mode:
#   a mention is someone asking. Channels not listed get no answers; an invite to one asks the PI.
#   People link their own Slack account (DM the bot `link github <h>` or `link gitlab <h>`), saved in
#   state/slack/people.json. `person` lines are only for people who can't or won't link themselves.
#
# person <workspace> <slack user id, e.g. UABCD1234> <github handle, or gitlab:<handle>>
#   (user id only: display names aren't unique and anyone can change theirs. Profile → ⋮ → Copy member ID.)
#   So agents know who is who. Instructions count only from the PI and the collaborators in the
#   project's AGENTS.md "People".

# workspace myworkspace
# pi myworkspace UXXXXXXXXXX
# channel myworkspace my-project-channel projects=my-project mode=draft posters=lead every=6h
